 Cell Trials\nSoft Tissue Surgery\nShow Results\nBG34-200 plus immunotherapy for canine malignant melanoma\nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the effectiveness of a new cancer immune therapy, BG34-200, for the treatment of canine malignant mel\nClinical, molecular, and immune characterization of naturally occurring osteosarcoma in dogs (COTC034)\nThe purpose of this study is to better understand the biology of primary bone cancer of the leg (appendicular osteosarcoma, OS) in dogs.\nSBRT for Canine Lung Tumor Study\nPrimary pulmonary carcinomas in dogs are most commonly treated with surgery, which can result in survival times of approximately one year.\nStereotactic ablative radiotherapy (SABR) for lung metastases\nThis study will evaluate whether a single, highly focused dose of radiation can safely treat cancer that has spread to the lungs in dogs.\nDefining Actionable Targets for Osteosarcoma\nOsteosarcoma represents a challenging disease to treat in both dogs and humans.\nEvaluation of TLR agonist Z-007 in dogs with cancer\nWhile significant improvements in immune based cancer treatments have been made, stimulating an effective immune response to most cancers remains a\nDose-escalation pilot study investigating whole abdomen radiation therapy for feline chronic inflammatory enteropathy\nWe are studying whether a low-dose radiation treatment to the whole abdomen can safely and effectively help cats with chronic inflammatory enteropa\nEvaluation of Kidney Pathology After Investigational Treatment in Dogs with Chronic Kidney Disease\nIn dogs with chronic kidney disease (CKD), the available medical management consists of supportive and symptomatic treatment with the goal of corre\nTreatment of canine soft tissue sarcoma with FAP CAR-T cells\nSoft tissue sarcoma (STS) represents a type of cancer that has been difficult to treat with immune-based therapies across multiple species, includi\nTesting small interfering RNA therapeutics for inflammatory skin conditions in companion dogs\nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the effectiveness of a new therapy for two different skin diseases in dogs: atopic dermatitis and cutaneou\nTreatment of Canine Metastatic Osteosarcoma with Combined Tumor Microenvironment Modification and Allogeneic Vaccination\nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the combination of a new tumor vaccine given in combination with an oral therapy designed to stimulate the\nOptimizing integration of veterinary clinical research findings with human health systems to improve strategies for early detection and interventions involving splenic hemangiosarcoma\nHemangiosarcoma (HSA) is a cancer of the cells lining the blood vessels that is very aggressive; it accounts for 5-7% of all cancers in dogs result\nPagination\nCurrent page\n1\nPage\n2\nNext page\n››\nLast page\nLast »\nFooter\nAccreditation\nAnnual Safety Report\nCareers\nComing to Campus\nDirectory\nWebster Family Library\nHospitals & Clinics\nFoster Hospital for Small Animals\nHospital for Large Animals\nLuke and Lily Lerner Clinic\nTufts Veterinary Field Service\nTufts Wildlife Clinic\nTufts at Tech\nVeterinary Diagnostic Laboratory\nContact\nCummings School of Veterinary Medicine at Tufts University\nJean Mayer Administration Building\n200 Westboro Road\nNorth Grafton, MA 01536\n© Tufts University 2026\nNon-Discrimination Statement\nPrivacy\nTufts Mobile",
